  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "The word is written x-ray or X-ray, always with a hyphen; the plural is x-rays. You take an x-ray (make the picture) or have an x-ray (go through the examination). The verb x-ray means take an x-ray of: they x-rayed her hand.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "The doctor made an x-ray of my hand.",
      "correct": "The doctor took an x-ray of my hand.",
      "note": "The verb for making the picture is take, as with a photograph."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": {
    "text": "The x is the letter that stands for an unknown amount in a calculation: the scientist who discovered the rays did not know what they were, and gave them this name in German, from which English translated it. Ray is the ordinary word for a thin line of light or energy.",
    "sources_consulted": [
      "Wiktionary",
      "Online Etymology Dictionary"
    ],
    "checked": "2026-09-16"
  },
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "In many languages the rays and the examination are named after the physicist who discovered them, and the picture has a word of its own. English uses x-ray for all three senses: the rays, the picture, and the examination.",
    "grammar": "Countable in all senses: an x-ray, two x-rays; the rays themselves are usually plural. Take an x-ray means make the picture; have an x-ray means be examined. Before a noun: an x-ray machine, the x-ray department.",
    "culture": "Airports and some public buildings use x-ray machines to check bags, and dentists take x-rays of teeth as a routine part of a checkup. A scan is a different kind of picture, made by other machines.",
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Two clear syllables with the stress on the first: EKS-ray. The letter x is pronounced eks, and the second syllable rhymes with day."
  },
